WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.438
Welcome to your Danish citizenship exam companion.

2
00:00:03.388 --> 00:00:09.288
This is Chapter Six, Theme Spotlights, Episode One: the geography of Denmark.

3
00:00:09.838 --> 00:00:12.438
Chapter Six is different from the others.

4
00:00:12.388 --> 00:00:19.825
It is a collection of themes, from geography and sights to the royal house, religion, schools, health and climate.

5
00:00:19.825 --> 00:00:21.930
We begin with the land itself.

6
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:31.305
The Danish landscape was shaped by the ice ages, which, in several rounds, completely covered the area that today makes up Denmark.

7
00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:34.884
The ice melted about 11,500 years ago.

8
00:00:35.434 --> 00:00:38.922
Denmark is one of the world's lowest-lying countries.

9
00:00:38.872 --> 00:00:43.559
On average, Denmark's area lies 34 metres above sea level.

10
00:00:43.559 --> 00:00:51.609
The highest natural point is called Møllehøj and lies in eastern Jutland, about 170 metres above sea level.

11
00:00:51.609 --> 00:01:03.006
Denmark's area is about 43,000 square kilometres, and is mostly covered by fertile, clayey and sandy soil, but in a few places the landscape is very different.

12
00:01:03.556 --> 00:01:11.681
On the eastern side of the island of Møn, for example, there is an almost 130-metre-high cliff, Møns Klint.

13
00:01:11.631 --> 00:01:20.056
Also the island of Bornholm and the group of islands Ertholmene, which lie out in the Baltic Sea, differ from the rest of the landscape.

14
00:01:20.056 --> 00:01:23.994
The islands are the only places in Denmark where you can see the bedrock.

15
00:01:23.994 --> 00:01:33.669
Denmark used to be covered by forest, but today about 60 percent of the country is cultivated, in Danish opdyrket, which is almost two thirds of the country.

16
00:01:33.668 --> 00:01:35.981
An exam may ask it as a fraction.

17
00:01:35.981 --> 00:01:45.960
In Danish, Jutland is Jylland, Zealand is Sjælland, Funen is Fyn, the North Sea is Nordsøen, and the Baltic Sea is Østersøen.

18
00:01:46.510 --> 00:01:52.698
Denmark consists of a peninsula, Jutland, and 443 named islands.

19
00:01:52.648 --> 00:01:58.810
But people live on only about 80 of the Danish islands, and the rest are small and uninhabited.

20
00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:01.185
The largest island is Zealand.

21
00:02:01.185 --> 00:02:04.885
South of Zealand lie the islands of Lolland and Falster.

22
00:02:04.885 --> 00:02:15.898
Because of the many islands, Denmark has a very long coastline, about 7,300 kilometres, and nowhere in Denmark is it more than 52 kilometres to the sea.

23
00:02:15.898 --> 00:02:19.434
The North Sea is the largest of the seas around Denmark.

24
00:02:19.984 --> 00:02:25.359
It lies off the Jutland west coast and is also called Vesterhavet, the West Sea.

25
00:02:25.309 --> 00:02:35.759
North of Zealand and Funen lies the water Kattegat, which in the south is connected to the Baltic Sea by the three straits Lillebælt, Storebælt and Øresund.

26
00:02:35.759 --> 00:02:46.428
It is only in South Jutland, the southernmost part of Jutland, that there is a fixed land border, of 68 kilometres, to a neighbouring country, and that is to Germany.

27
00:02:46.978 --> 00:02:54.666
The climate in Denmark is especially marked by the location by the North Sea and the short distance to the coast everywhere in the country.

28
00:02:54.616 --> 00:03:03.066
From the Gulf of Mexico, a permanent stream of warm water, the so-called Gulf Stream, is carried towards Northern Europe.

29
00:03:03.066 --> 00:03:17.303
Because of the Gulf Stream, the climate in north-western Europe, and particularly in winter, is markedly milder than the climate in other places in the world that lie just as far north, for example Moscow or southern Alaska.

30
00:03:17.303 --> 00:03:29.591
The coldest months, January and February, have an average daytime temperature of three to four degrees, while the warmest, July and August, average about 22 degrees.

31
00:03:29.591 --> 00:03:33.378
It can however become both considerably colder and warmer.

32
00:03:33.378 --> 00:03:44.382
The lowest temperature ever measured in Denmark is minus 31.2, in 1982, while the highest is 36.4, in 1975.

33
00:03:44.932 --> 00:03:46.695
Next, the bridges.

34
00:03:46.644 --> 00:03:50.320
Denmark is today connected by several bridges and tunnels.

35
00:03:50.320 --> 00:04:01.570
In 1935, the first bridge over the Little Belt, Lillebælt, between Jutland and Funen was inaugurated, and in 1970 another Little Belt Bridge opened.

36
00:04:01.570 --> 00:04:14.369
In 1997 the Great Belt Link, Storebæltsforbindelsen, between Funen and Zealand was inaugurated for rail traffic, and the year after, in 1998, for car traffic.

37
00:04:14.369 --> 00:04:27.264
In the year 2000, with the Øresund Link, the Øresundsbroen, the Øresund Bridge, a fixed connection was created over the water of Øresund between Zealand and the neighbouring country Sweden.

38
00:04:27.814 --> 00:04:34.014
Denmark's capital and largest city is Copenhagen, which lies on the eastern part of Zealand.

39
00:04:33.964 --> 00:04:44.082
Today about one third of Denmark's population lives in the capital area, that is, in Copenhagen itself and the municipalities around Copenhagen.

40
00:04:44.632 --> 00:04:50.344
Aarhus, with about 300,000 inhabitants, is the largest city in Jutland.

41
00:04:50.294 --> 00:04:54.882
It lies on Jutland's east coast and is Denmark's second largest city.

42
00:04:54.882 --> 00:05:00.169
The largest city on Funen is Odense, with about 200,000 inhabitants.

43
00:05:00.169 --> 00:05:02.707
It is the country's third largest city.

44
00:05:02.707 --> 00:05:11.070
Denmark's fourth largest city is Aalborg, with about 125,000 inhabitants, which lies in North Jutland.

45
00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:17.220
The fifth largest is Esbjerg, with about 75,000 inhabitants, in West Jutland.

46
00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:26.870
The most important raw materials in Denmark have in recent years been the oil and natural gas that lie in the seabed under the Danish part of the North Sea.

47
00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:33.070
Since the end of the 1990s, Denmark has exported more energy than the country has imported.

48
00:05:33.070 --> 00:05:41.670
The extraction of oil and natural gas therefore has relatively great importance for the Danish economy, even though its importance has been falling.

49
00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:47.957
In December 2020, the Folketing decided to end this production by 2050 at the latest.

50
00:05:47.957 --> 00:05:53.550
This is due, among other things, to a wish for a green transition to renewable energy.

51
00:05:54.100 --> 00:05:56.350
Here is your recap for Episode One.

52
00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:59.925
The ice melted about 11,500 years ago.

53
00:05:59.925 --> 00:06:06.012
Denmark is one of the world's lowest-lying countries, on average 34 metres above sea level.

54
00:06:06.012 --> 00:06:12.800
The highest natural point is Møllehøj, about 170 metres, in eastern Jutland.

55
00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:19.000
The area is about 43,000 square kilometres, and about 60 percent is cultivated.

56
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:22.537
Møns Klint is almost 130 metres high.

57
00:06:22.537 --> 00:06:26.150
Bedrock can only be seen on Bornholm and Ertholmene.

58
00:06:26.150 --> 00:06:33.287
Denmark has the peninsula of Jutland and 443 named islands, about 80 of them inhabited.

59
00:06:33.287 --> 00:06:35.662
The largest island is Zealand.

60
00:06:35.662 --> 00:06:42.762
The coastline is about 7,300 kilometres, and nowhere is more than 52 kilometres from the sea.

61
00:06:42.762 --> 00:06:45.875
The North Sea is also called Vesterhavet.

62
00:06:45.875 --> 00:06:51.575
Kattegat connects to the Baltic Sea through Lillebælt, Storebælt and Øresund.

63
00:06:51.575 --> 00:06:55.537
The only land border is to Germany, 68 kilometres.

64
00:06:55.537 --> 00:07:03.812
The first bridge over Lillebælt, between Jutland and Funen, opened in 1935, and a second in 1970.

65
00:07:03.812 --> 00:07:16.737
The Great Belt Link, the motorway link across Storebælt, opened in 1997 for rail and 1998 for cars, and the Øresund Bridge, Øresundsbroen, in 2000.

66
00:07:16.737 --> 00:07:22.562
The largest cities are Copenhagen, Aarhus, Odense, Aalborg and Esbjerg.

67
00:07:22.562 --> 00:07:28.512
The Folketing decided in December 2020 to end oil and gas production by 2050.

68
00:07:29.062 --> 00:07:31.662
Now it is time for your exam practice.

69
00:07:31.612 --> 00:07:36.524
These are real questions from past Danish citizenship exams on what you just heard.

70
00:07:36.524 --> 00:07:44.010
For each one, I will read the question and the choices, then pause so you can pick your answer, and then I will give you the correct answer.

71
00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:45.810
Question 1.

72
00:07:45.760 --> 00:07:49.660
In which year was the motorway link across Storebælt inaugurated?

73
00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:52.410
Option A: 1986.

74
00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.885
Option B: 1998.

75
00:07:54.885 --> 00:07:57.060
Option C: 2010.
[DA] I hvilket år blev motorvejsforbindelsen over Storebælt indviet?  A: I 1986  |  B: I 1998  |  C: I 2010

76
00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:06.297
The answer is B: 1998.

77
00:08:06.247 --> 00:08:08.862
It appeared in the June 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: I 1998

78
00:08:09.412 --> 00:08:10.662
Question 2.

79
00:08:10.612 --> 00:08:14.712
In which year was the Øresund Bridge, Øresundsbroen, opened?

80
00:08:14.712 --> 00:08:17.124
Option A: 1970.

81
00:08:17.124 --> 00:08:19.737
Option B: 1985.

82
00:08:19.737 --> 00:08:21.744
Option C: 2000.
[DA] I hvilket år blev Øresundsbroen åbnet?  A: I 1970  |  B: I 1985  |  C: I 2000

83
00:08:28.294 --> 00:08:30.456
The answer is C: 2000.

84
00:08:30.406 --> 00:08:33.090
It appeared in the November 2020 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: I 2000

85
00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:34.977
Question 3.

86
00:08:34.928 --> 00:08:39.165
How large a part of Denmark is today cultivated agricultural land?

87
00:08:39.165 --> 00:08:41.803
Option A: Almost one sixth.

88
00:08:41.803 --> 00:08:44.178
Option B: Almost one third.

89
00:08:44.178 --> 00:08:46.740
Option C: Almost two thirds.
[DA] Hvor stor en del af Danmark er i dag opdyrket landbrugsland?  A: Næsten 1/6  |  B: Næsten 1/3  |  C: Næsten 2/3

90
00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.915
The answer is C: Almost two thirds.

91
00:08:55.865 --> 00:08:58.710
It appeared in the June 2021 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Næsten 2/3

92
00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:00.560
Question 4.

93
00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:03.398
How high is Denmark's highest natural point?

94
00:09:03.398 --> 00:09:07.673
Option A: About 170 metres above sea level.

95
00:09:07.673 --> 00:09:11.885
Option B: About 470 metres above sea level.

96
00:09:11.885 --> 00:09:16.152
Option C: About 770 metres above sea level.
[DA] Hvor højt er Danmarks højeste naturlige punkt?  A: Cirka 170 meter over havets overflade  |  B: Cirka 470 meter over havets overflade  |  C: Cirka 770 meter over havets overflade

97
00:09:22.702 --> 00:09:27.065
The answer is A: About 170 metres above sea level.

98
00:09:27.015 --> 00:09:32.970
It appeared in the November 2021, May 2023 and May 2025 exams.
[DA] ✔ A: Cirka 170 meter over havets overflade

99
00:09:33.520 --> 00:09:34.983
Question 5.

100
00:09:34.933 --> 00:09:38.058
Where is Denmark's highest natural point located?

101
00:09:38.058 --> 00:09:40.445
Option A: In North Zealand.

102
00:09:40.445 --> 00:09:43.033
Option B: In South Jutland.

103
00:09:43.033 --> 00:09:45.396
Option C: In East Jutland.
[DA] Hvor ligger Danmarks højeste naturlige punkt?  A: I Nordsjælland  |  B: I Sønderjylland  |  C: I Østjylland

104
00:09:51.946 --> 00:09:54.534
The answer is C: In East Jutland.

105
00:09:54.484 --> 00:09:57.390
It appeared in the November 2022 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: I Østjylland

106
00:09:57.940 --> 00:09:59.378
Question 6.

107
00:09:59.328 --> 00:10:02.828
Over which water did a bridge open in 1935?

108
00:10:02.828 --> 00:10:05.078
Option A: Øresund.

109
00:10:05.078 --> 00:10:07.265
Option B: Storebælt.

110
00:10:07.265 --> 00:10:09.384
Option C: Lillebælt.
[DA] Over hvilket farvand åbnede en bro i 1935?  A: Øresund  |  B: Storebælt  |  C: Lillebælt

111
00:10:15.934 --> 00:10:18.147
The answer is C: Lillebælt.

112
00:10:18.097 --> 00:10:21.066
It appeared in the November 2025 exam.
[DA] ✔ C: Lillebælt

113
00:10:21.616 --> 00:10:23.054
Question 7.

114
00:10:23.004 --> 00:10:26.054
Which of the following cities has the most inhabitants?

115
00:10:26.054 --> 00:10:28.366
Option A: Esbjerg.

116
00:10:28.366 --> 00:10:30.266
Option B: Aarhus.

117
00:10:30.266 --> 00:10:32.124
Option C: Aalborg.
[DA] Hvilken af følgende byer har flest indbyggere?  A: Esbjerg  |  B: Aarhus  |  C: Aalborg

118
00:10:38.674 --> 00:10:40.824
The answer is B: Aarhus.

119
00:10:40.774 --> 00:10:43.686
It appeared in the June 2026 exam.
[DA] ✔ B: Aarhus

120
00:10:44.236 --> 00:10:45.999
That's the end of this part.

121
00:10:45.949 --> 00:10:47.016
Well done.

